notes               = "One poem running across two printed pages: the dedication and the opening are on 165, the date on 166. The dedication با یاد حسین کاظمی ('in memory of Hossein Kazemi') is printed in the outer margin — the painter Hossein Kazemi (۱۳۰۳-۱۳۷۵ / 1924-1996), a founder of Iranian modernism, who died in the year this was written, which is why the dedication is با یاد and not به. سنگ‌های رنگی‌اش, 'his coloured stones', points at the paintings. TWO PUNS CARRY THE POEM AND NEITHER SURVIVES INTO ENGLISH: (1) مهر, love or kindness, against مُهر, a seal — the same letters — in the first two lines; (2) زنده‌یاد, literally 'living memory', is the ordinary Persian honorific for the dead, 'the late'. The old man says he would rather be شاد (glad) than زنده یاد (late), and on page 166 he becomes exactly that. The lantern keeps the honorific in quotation marks so the turn stays visible. Khordad is month 3 → +621 = 1996. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

مهر مردِ مهربان
مُهرِ خامُشی به لب نهاد،
مثلِ سنگ‌های رنگی‌اش، صبور شد
باغبان باغ‌های نور شد...

پیرمرد...
قطره‌های جام را تهی سرکشید...
و گفت: در هوای اتفاقِ سرخ شاد باشم
به از آن که در کویرِ کور
زنده یاد باشم.
...دور از وطن...
سال‌ها گذشت...

بینِ ما...
هر شبی که قرص مَه تمام بود
گفتگوی بی‌کلام بود...
پیرمرد... مثل گل میانِ سنگ،
بی‌درنگ... در هوای سرخِ درد ریشه کرد...
ریشه‌های درد
استخوان به استخوان... مثل باغ...
در زمین زبرِ پیکَرش نشست...
پیرمرد... ساکت و صبور و... سرد
لب به ساغر تُهی نهاد و گفت...
در هوای اتفاقِ سرخ شاد باشم
به از آن که در کویرِ کور
زنده‌یاد باشم...
باغ درد... شاد شد...
پیرمرد... زنده یاد شد...
مهر مردِ مهربانِ شاد
مهرِ خامشی زِ لب گشود
بر نفس نهاد...
تهران، خرداد ۱۳۷۵

===machine===

The kindness of the kind man
laid the seal of silence on his lip;
like his coloured stones, he turned patient,
he became the gardener of the gardens of light...

The old man...
drained the last drops of the cup...
and said: better to be glad in the air of the red chance
than, out in the blind desert,
to be "of blessed memory."
...far from home...
the years went by...

Between us...
on every night the moon's disc was full
there was a conversation without words...
The old man... like a flower among stones,
without pausing... took root in the red air of pain...
The roots of pain,
bone upon bone... like a garden...
settled into the rough ground of his body...
The old man... silent and patient and... cold,
set his lip to the empty goblet and said...
better to be glad in the air of the red chance
than, out in the blind desert,
to be "of blessed memory"...
The garden of pain... turned glad...
The old man... became "of blessed memory"...
The kindness of the kind and glad man
loosed the seal of silence from his lip
and laid it on his breath...
Tehran, Khordad 1375

===lantern===

The kindness of the kind man
laid the seal of silence on his lip.
Like his coloured stones he turned patient;
he became the gardener of the gardens of light.

The old man
drained the last drops of the cup
and said: better to be glad in the air of the red chance
than, out in the blind desert,
to be "of blessed memory."
...far from home...
the years went by...

Between us,
on every night the moon's disc was full,
there was a conversation without words.
The old man, like a flower among stones,
took root at once in the red air of pain.
The roots of pain,
bone upon bone, like a garden,
settled into the rough ground of his body.
The old man — silent, patient, and cold —
set his lip to the empty goblet and said:
better to be glad in the air of the red chance
than, out in the blind desert,
to be "of blessed memory."
The garden of pain turned glad.
The old man became "of blessed memory."
The kindness of the kind and glad man
loosed the seal of silence from his lip
and laid it on his breath...

Tehran, Khordad 1375

===translation===

===footnotes===

زنده‌یاد means, word for word, *living memory*, and is what Persian calls someone who has
died — the equivalent of 'the late'. The painter in this poem says twice that he would rather be
glad than be زنده یاد, and the poem's last movement grants him the thing he refused. English has no
honorific with a literal meaning that pleasant, so it is kept in quotation marks here.

The opening two lines also rhyme on a pun that cannot cross: مهر is kindness or love, مُهر is a
seal, and they are written with the same letters. The kindness of the kind man is what seals his
mouth.
